WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.390
Good morning, Hank.

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:02.390
It's Tuesday.

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:05.860
So you've started a lot of businesses: Crash
Course, Scishow, DFTBA Records, VidCon, the

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.779
ceaseless juggernaut that is 2D Glasses.

00:00:07.779 --> 00:00:11.230
And Hank, your companies employ dozens of
people, none of whom work for the federally

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.019
mandated minimum wage of 7 dollars 25 cents
per hour.

00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:18.280
But Hank, let's imagine that your next project
is a fast food restaurant, "Corndogs and Sodium".

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.760
What impact would raising the federal minimum
wage have on you and your employees?

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.500
At first glance it seems like a no brainer:
any minimum wage is terrible, both for "Corndogs

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.390
and Sodium" and for its employees.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:33.269
The Econ 101 argument goes like this: the
free market is going to set wages where they

00:00:33.269 --> 00:00:37.500
need to be, like if you want to pay 5 dollars
an hour for "Corndogs and Sodium" employees,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.199
but no one takes the job for 5 dollars an
hour, you're gonna have to pay more.

00:00:41.199 --> 00:00:44.710
You'll increase your wages until you can attract
the kind of employees that you need to, you

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:48.289
know, batter and fry and serve encased, cast-off
pig meat.

00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:51.770
And we know that economies tend to grow less
when governments set and control prices, so

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.800
higher minimum wages restrict economic growth.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.510
Plus, unemployment will go up because of minimum
wage is 10 dollars per hour, "Corndogs and

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.370
Sodium" could only afford to hire one person.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:03.819
But if there was an unrestricted wage market,
then they could attract two people who'd be

00:01:03.819 --> 00:01:05.930
willing to work for 5 dollars an hour each.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:10.830
So in the end, setting a minimum wage is an
attempt to alleviate poverty, that actually

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:11.830
increases it.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:15.980
However Hank, surprisingly enough, it turns
out that actual labour markets are a lot more

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:19.800
complex than the models of labor markets created
by college freshmen.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.930
This brings us to a famous study by two economists,
David Card and Alan Kreuger.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:28.850
So in 1992, the state of New Jersey raised
its minimum wage 18.8 percent.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.520
Pennsylvania, right next door, did not raise
its minimum wage.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.940
Card and Krueger had the bright idea to go
to the border of New Jersey and Pennsylvania,

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.050
and do employment surveys on either side of
it.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:42.270
And what they found is that restaurant employment
in New Jersey actually increased when the

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:43.360
minimum wage went up.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.110
Since then, a bunch of other studies have
confirmed Card and Krueger's findings, while

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.910
some have found that there actually are negative
effects to employment when you raise the minimum

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:52.460
wage, although it's surprisingly and consistently
mild.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:53.460
Why?

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.460
Well, a bunch of reasons.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.270
For one, the minimum wage is probably near
where the market would set it.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.920
But also, low-wage workers tend to spend most
of their pay raises, which leads to increased

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.770
economic activity, which in turn leads to
more jobs.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:08.180
And higher wages also mean less turnover,
which leads to lower costs of training, and

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:09.180
hiring, and firing.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.439
On the downside, higher wages are also associated
with higher prices on goods and services that

00:02:13.439 --> 00:02:17.579
rely on low-wage labor, which means that your
corndogs, Hank, would probably be a little

00:02:17.579 --> 00:02:18.579
bit more expensive.

00:02:18.579 --> 00:02:22.829
So Hank, the larger question is whether raising
the minimum wage actually reduces poverty.

00:02:22.829 --> 00:02:26.940
And on that front, there is growing consensus
that at least in the medium run, it does.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.920
A number of big recent studies have shown
that raising the minimum wage 10 percent reduces

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.359
the number of people in poverty by about 2.5
percent.

00:02:34.359 --> 00:02:37.989
Even many opponents of the minimum wage acknowledge
this, but it's important to know that like,

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:39.379
that won't always work.

00:02:39.379 --> 00:02:43.379
At some point, raising the minimum wage will
lead to inflation and slower job creation.

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:45.390
It's just not clear where that point is.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:49.170
But it's just as disingenuous to call the
minimum wage a "job-killer", as it is to say

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.191
that the minimum wage is gonna fix economic
inequality.

00:02:51.191 --> 00:02:55.069
In short Hank, in economics, there's no such
thing as a "free lunch", but when it comes

00:02:55.069 --> 00:03:00.129
to reducing poverty without affecting employment,
higher minimum wages seem at least to be the

00:03:00.129 --> 00:03:01.730
cheapest lunch available.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:05.879
But ultimately, Hank, now that I'm, I guess,
an employer, I'm more persuaded by the personal

00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:06.879
argument.

00:03:06.879 --> 00:03:09.959
We found that paying a living wage, which
we would do even if we opened "Corndogs and

00:03:09.959 --> 00:03:12.640
Sodium", leads to happier, more productive
employees.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.760
Now, I know that's hard to quantify, but it's
also what's allowed VidCon and DFTBA Records

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.730
to retain employees for years and years, and
grow sustainably.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:24.439
Now Hank, obviously I am not an economist,
although I did win a bronze medal in Economics

00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:28.300
at the Alabama State Academic Decathlon tournament
in 1993, but our strategy has worked out pretty

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.569
well for us so far, and it's also working
in much larger companies like Costco.

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:35.250
Hank, the United States is a rich country,
and I think there's a growing body of evidence

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.860
that the US doesn't benefit from having poor
workers.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.859
Of course raising the minimum wage isn't gonna
fix that problem, but I hope at least we can

00:03:41.859 --> 00:03:45.079
begin to have a nuanced conversation about
the problem.

00:03:45.079 --> 00:03:46.260
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

